    {
      "id": "cest-vs-il-y-a",
      "title": "C'est vs il y a",
      "minutes": 9,
      "lede": "Both can be translated as “it is” or “there is”, but they do completely different jobs.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "tip",
          "title": "The rule in one line",
          "text": "Use c'est to identify, describe or point at a specific thing (“this is…”). Use il y a to say that something exists in a place (“there is / there are…”)."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "title": "Quick comparison",
          "headers": [
            "",
            "C'est",
            "Il y a"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Job",
              "Identifies or points out a specific thing or person",
              "Says that something exists or is present in a place"
            ],
            [
              "English",
              "it is · this is · that is",
              "there is · there are · there's"
            ],
            [
              "Plural",
              "ce sont",
              "never changes"
            ],
            [
              "What follows",
              "something known: le / la / l' / les, mon / ma, a name",
              "something new: un / une / des, a number, a quantity"
            ],
            [
              "Answers",
              "Qu'est-ce que c'est ? Qui est-ce ?",
              "Qu'est-ce qu'il y a ?"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "info",
          "title": "Same place, two different sentences",
          "text": "C'est le Vieux-Port. = This is the Old Port (I'm pointing at one specific place we both know). Il y a un port à Marseille. = A port exists in Marseille (I'm telling you it's there). Notice the articles: le (known) after c'est, un (new) after il y a."
        },
        {
          "type": "phrases",
          "title": "Listen and compare",
          "items": [
            {
              "fr": "C'est le Vieux-Port."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Il y a un port à Marseille."
            },
            {
              "fr": "C'est ma ville."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Il y a trois musées dans ma ville."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Ce sont mes cousins."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Il y a des cousins à la maison."
            },
            {
              "fr": "Il n'y a pas de parc ici."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "note",
          "tone": "warning",
          "title": "Watch out",
          "text": "1) In the negative, il y a becomes il n'y a pas de + noun: Il n'y a pas de parc ici. 2) Il y a followed by a time (il y a deux ans) means “ago”. That is a different use, and you will meet it with the past tense."
        },
        {
          "type": "exercise",
          "title": "C'est, ce sont or il y a ?",
          "items": [
            {
              "prompt": "___ ma ville.",
              "answers": [
                "C'est"
              ],
              "choices": [
                "C'est",
                "Ce sont",
                "Il y a"
              ]
            },
            {
              "prompt": "___ un parc dans mon quartier.",
              "answers": [
                "Il y a"
              ],
              "choices": [
                "C'est",
                "Ce sont",
                "Il y a"
              ]
            },
            {
              "prompt": "___ mes cousins.",
              "answers": [
                "Ce sont"
              ],
              "choices": [
                "C'est",
                "Ce sont",
                "Il y a"
              ]
            },
            {
              "prompt": "Dans la rue, ___ deux cafés.",
              "answers": [
                "il y a"
              ],
              "choices": [
                "C'est",
                "Ce sont",
                "Il y a"
              ]
            },
            {
              "prompt": "Là, ___ le musée.",
